WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.873 --> 00:00:09.039
The courtroom was silent with tension as the presiding judge glared at the proud woman standing before him.

2
00:00:09.980 --> 00:00:12.782
She had been arrested and tried, and the verdict was final.

3
00:00:13.783 --> 00:00:14.624
She was found guilty.

4
00:00:15.164 --> 00:00:15.625
Her crime?

5
00:00:16.345 --> 00:00:17.366
Voting in an election.

6
00:00:18.487 --> 00:00:22.931
At the time of the trial, in 1873, it was against the law for women to vote in

7
00:00:22.951 --> 00:00:23.711
the United States.

8
00:00:24.672 --> 00:00:29.688
Women had no voice in how their government was run, no say in how laws were made, and

9
00:00:29.748 --> 00:00:32.029
no way to state their grievances against the government.

10
00:00:33.070 --> 00:00:33.490
Susan B.

11
00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:38.052
Anthony was born on February 15, 1820, on a farm in Massachusetts.

12
00:00:39.212 --> 00:00:46.535
She was raised in a Quaker family whose members were abolitionists—antislavery activists—and they profoundly affected Anthony's sense of

13
00:00:46.595 --> 00:00:48.536
justice and equality from a young age.

14
00:00:49.536 --> 00:00:54.378
Other abolitionists met at her family's farm almost every Sunday to fight for the freedom of black slaves.

15
00:00:55.499 --> 00:01:00.777
Among those who came to her home was Frederick Douglass, a runaway slave who became a prominent abolitionist

16
00:01:00.857 --> 00:01:02.919
and political leader.

17
00:01:02.979 --> 00:01:09.202
In 1846, at the age of 26, Anthony became head of the Female Department at Canajoharie Academy in

18
00:01:09.222 --> 00:01:09.622
New York.

19
00:01:10.603 --> 00:01:15.985
During her two years there, she experienced the inequality that all female teachers faced in the educational system

20
00:01:16.025 --> 00:01:16.526
at the time.

21
00:01:17.606 --> 00:01:22.149
Female teachers were not as respected as male teachers, and they were paid less than men for the

22
00:01:22.189 --> 00:01:22.669
same work.

23
00:01:23.609 --> 00:01:29.177
Even more unfair was that most young girls in America did not receive an adequate education; many received

24
00:01:29.217 --> 00:01:29.677
none at all.

25
00:01:30.778 --> 00:01:33.280
After her experience at Canajoharie, Susan B.

26
00:01:33.320 --> 00:01:37.423
Anthony was inspired to publicly argue for equal opportunities for men and women.

27
00:01:38.664 --> 00:01:42.567
This concept was highly controversial at the time, as was advocating it in public.

28
00:01:43.688 --> 00:01:48.512
Anthony felt that all persons, regardless of their gender, should be allowed to attend any school, college, or

29
00:01:48.572 --> 00:01:54.712
university. Anthony believed that there was no difference between the male mind and the female mind, so she

30
00:01:54.772 --> 00:01:59.473
also advocated for coeducation and for men and women to be taught the same academic subjects.

31
00:02:00.374 --> 00:02:01.994
However, her battle was not easy.

32
00:02:02.994 --> 00:02:04.495
Many male educators laughed at her.

33
00:02:05.495 --> 00:02:11.077
But to foreshadow what was to come in education, in 1855 the University of Iowa became the first

34
00:02:11.157 --> 00:02:14.198
public university to admit both genders on an equal basis.

35
00:02:15.018 --> 00:02:20.039
During the initial stage of her campaign for equal education, Anthony quickly became aware of how a woman's

36
00:02:20.079 --> 00:02:22.400
role in society was not unlike that of a slave.

37
00:02:23.324 --> 00:02:26.006
Inequality in education was just one part of the problem.

38
00:02:26.786 --> 00:02:28.107
From then on, Susan B.

39
00:02:28.167 --> 00:02:32.549
Anthony devoted her life to fighting for women's rights and found other men and women who felt the

40
00:02:32.569 --> 00:02:33.010
same way.

41
00:02:34.431 --> 00:02:40.754
In 1851, while in Seneca, New York, Anthony was introduced to Elizabeth Cady Stanton, one of the leaders

42
00:02:40.814 --> 00:02:42.175
of the new women's rights movement.

43
00:02:42.995 --> 00:02:45.957
They quickly developed a strong partnership that would last many years.

44
00:02:47.098 --> 00:02:51.860
Anthony began wearing bloomers, baggy pants that were gathered at the ankles, a style of dress that was

45
00:02:51.901 --> 00:02:54.081
considered unladylike by many men and women.

46
00:02:55.082 --> 00:02:56.923
But wearing bloomers was a way for Susan B.

47
00:02:56.963 --> 00:03:02.365
Anthony to protest the status quo in society, and many early feminists loved to show off their new

48
00:03:02.405 --> 00:03:03.385
views through fashion.

49
00:03:04.226 --> 00:03:08.908
In 1852, Anthony attended her first woman's rights convention in Syracuse, New York.

50
00:03:09.848 --> 00:03:15.771
After that, she and Stanton tirelessly campaigned door to door, in legislatures, and in many town meetings for

51
00:03:15.791 --> 00:03:16.831
the cause of women's rights.

52
00:03:18.012 --> 00:03:19.048
However, Susan B.

53
00:03:19.108 --> 00:03:24.130
Anthony began to realize a fundamental truth: for women to make a difference in public affairs and in

54
00:03:24.150 --> 00:03:27.292
their homes, they needed women's suffrage—the power to vote.

55
00:03:28.812 --> 00:03:35.615
In 1866, Anthony and Stanton founded the American Equal Rights Association to try to win voting rights for

56
00:03:35.675 --> 00:03:37.716
everyone, regardless of color or sex.

57
00:03:38.776 --> 00:03:45.119
In addition, two years later, the two women founded The Revolution, a newspaper whose aim was to secure

58
00:03:45.159 --> 00:03:48.932
the right to vote for women and African-Americans.

59
00:03:48.972 --> 00:03:54.836
The paper also discussed issues such as equal pay for equal work, divorce laws, and the church's position

60
00:03:54.876 --> 00:03:55.596
on women's rights.

61
00:03:56.837 --> 00:04:02.780
Soon, bloomer-wearing Anthony was recognized as the most famous advocate of the movement, and as a result, she

62
00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:04.361
became a target of public abuse.

63
00:04:05.682 --> 00:04:10.405
Elizabeth Stanton's public image was more feminine and demure; she was a wife and mother.

64
00:04:11.625 --> 00:04:15.848
In contrast, Anthony remained unmarried and was portrayed as a cold, hard person.

65
00:04:16.908 --> 00:04:22.612
But Anthony did not allow public opinion to sway her; she tirelessly traveled and spoke at numerous halls

66
00:04:22.672 --> 00:04:25.794
and town meetings, never losing her focus on what she was fighting for.

67
00:04:27.135 --> 00:04:32.899
In 1868, the United States passed the Fourteenth Amendment, which stated that all people born in America were

68
00:04:32.939 --> 00:04:36.282
citizens and no legal privileges could be denied to any citizen.

69
00:04:37.343 --> 00:04:43.427
But section two of the amendment, by specifying African-American males as citizens, seemed to cast doubt on whether

70
00:04:43.467 --> 00:04:44.768
women were actually citizens.

71
00:04:45.684 --> 00:04:46.084
Susan B.

72
00:04:46.144 --> 00:04:50.345
Anthony decided to challenge this amendment and urged other women to do the same by voting in the

73
00:04:50.385 --> 00:04:51.005
next election.

74
00:04:52.306 --> 00:04:55.727
Women in ten states went to vote, but many were turned away at the polls.

75
00:04:57.167 --> 00:05:02.589
But on November 5, 1872, Anthony voted in a presidential election in Rochester, New York.

76
00:05:03.489 --> 00:05:06.490
At the voting booth, she claimed she had the right to vote under the U.S.

77
00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:08.250
Constitution and the Fourteenth Amendment.

78
00:05:09.330 --> 00:05:12.371
No one tried to stop her, but four days later, she was arrested.

79
00:05:13.592 --> 00:05:13.992
Susan B.

80
00:05:14.052 --> 00:05:15.132
Anthony was found guilty.

81
00:05:15.904 --> 00:05:18.827
Before sentencing her, the judge asked if she had anything to say.

82
00:05:19.867 --> 00:05:22.550
Anthony courageously stated she had a great many things to say.

83
00:05:23.671 --> 00:05:30.176
Standing up, she said in a calm voice: "My natural rights, my civil rights, my political rights, my

84
00:05:30.256 --> 00:05:32.618
judicial rights, are all alike ignored.

85
00:05:33.579 --> 00:05:38.483
Robbed of the fundamental privilege of citizenship, I am degraded from the status of a citizen to that

86
00:05:38.523 --> 00:05:44.328
of a subject; and not only myself individually, but all of my sex, are, by your honor's verdict,

87
00:05:44.708 --> 00:05:48.430
doomed to political subjection under this, so-called, form of government."

88
00:05:49.610 --> 00:05:52.851
The judge tried to silence her and ordered her to pay a $100 fine.

89
00:05:53.552 --> 00:05:54.612
Anthony refused to pay it.

90
00:05:55.513 --> 00:05:56.753
After her trial, Susan B.

91
00:05:56.793 --> 00:06:00.054
Anthony was impelled to fight harder for her rights.

92
00:06:00.094 --> 00:06:06.757
In 1877, she gathered petitions from 26 states with 10,000 signatures of people demanding women's suffrage.

93
00:06:08.138 --> 00:06:09.818
Anthony took those petitions to the U.S.

94
00:06:09.858 --> 00:06:12.199
Congress, only to be laughed at by the lawmakers.

95
00:06:13.060 --> 00:06:14.452
Still, she did not give up.

96
00:06:15.273 --> 00:06:20.534
She continued to appear before every session of Congress until 1906, the year of her death, to petition

97
00:06:20.574 --> 00:06:21.654
for a woman's right to vote.

98
00:06:22.715 --> 00:06:23.575
By then, Susan B.

99
00:06:23.615 --> 00:06:25.695
Anthony's public image had changed drastically.

100
00:06:26.436 --> 00:06:27.796
To many, she had become a heroine.

101
00:06:29.016 --> 00:06:31.517
Despite her undivided zeal for the cause, Susan B.

102
00:06:31.557 --> 00:06:34.498
Anthony did not see women's suffrage realized during her lifetime.

103
00:06:35.598 --> 00:06:41.360
She died on March 13, 1906, and her last public words were, "Failure is impossible!"

104
00:06:42.368 --> 00:06:47.011
This became the motto for future advocates of women's rights, who would carry on the campaign for justice

105
00:06:47.111 --> 00:06:47.771
and equality.

106
00:06:49.332 --> 00:06:53.494
In August 1920, the United States ratified the Nineteenth Amendment to the U.S.

107
00:06:53.514 --> 00:06:55.475
Constitution, which allowed women to vote.

108
00:06:56.416 --> 00:06:58.397
This amendment is also known as the Susan B.

109
00:06:58.457 --> 00:07:01.959
Anthony Amendment to honor the woman who dedicated her life to women's rights.

110
00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:06.041
Anthony was the voice that spoke when others were afraid or indifferent.

111
00:07:07.002 --> 00:07:11.486
She lived and died for what she strongly believed in, and more importantly, Susan B.

112
00:07:11.526 --> 00:07:13.832
Anthony proved that women have the right to make a difference.
